// drive.cla: the front-end-neutral compile/emit pipeline, extracted out of
// main.cla's `on App.startCLI` (Task 6, mac-resident-clarusc phase) so a
// future Mac-resident GUI front end (macgui, later tasks in this phase) can
// drive the SAME pipeline without a CLI shell around it. Pure code motion:
// every front end (today: main.cla's host CLI) must see byte-identical
// behavior to before this file existed -- see main.cla's own `feReadSource`
// for the seam a front end plugs into.
//
// A front end drives this file as: set `hostPaths`/`wantEmit`/`want68k`
// (below) for the invocation at hand, call `driveCompile`, and on success
// either read `combined` itself (appinfo-style metadata) or call
// `driveEmit68k` (or, C target, do its own shakeProgram+emitProgram --
// main.cla still owns that fork, see its own startCLI).

// hostPaths: true selects '/' path semantics (main.cla, the host CLI);
// false selects HFS ':' semantics (Task 8: drivePathJoin/driveKeyResolve
// + expand()'s Mac arm) for the future macgui front end (Task 10, which
// sets it false and supplies the resource-backed feReadSource). main.cla
// still sets it true unconditionally, so every hostPaths=false branch
// added below is dead code on host today -- exercised for real only by
// Task 11's on-Mac compile.
var hostPaths: bool

// wantEmit: the front end sets this true, before calling driveCompile,
// exactly when it intends to lower the checked program to IR afterward
// (main.cla's `emit`/`emit68k` subcommands both do). It gates the
// runtime-manifest splice + second checkProgram + lowerProgram stage
// inside driveCompile, reproducing startCLI's original
// `if emitMode or emitMode68k { ... }` gate now that check-only and
// appinfo callers share the same driveCompile entry point. Left at its
// zero value (false) for check-only/appinfo callers: neither one needs,
// or historically ever paid for, a runtime-module resolution.
var wantEmit: bool

// want68k: the front end additionally sets this true for the emit68k
// target specifically (never the C `emit` target) -- gates native.cla's
// manifest entry and the app raw-name/icon-path resolution below, exactly
// mirroring startCLI's original nested `if emitMode68k { ... }`.
var want68k: bool

// combined is the FIRST-pass assembled decl chain: Phase A's post-order
// assembly, after the --testapi early splice (driveEarlySplice) if that
// ran, but BEFORE the runtime-manifest splice (driveManifestSplice).
// Exposed as a global so a front end can still read it once driveCompile
// returns (main.cla's appinfoMode block scans it for the app section,
// exactly as the pre-split code did) and so driveManifestSplice can
// resolve the app's raw name/icon path from the same chain the original
// code read them from.
var combined: int

// combined2 is the manifest-spliced chain lowerProgram actually runs over
// -- combined itself when driveManifestSplice added nothing (or wantEmit
// was never set). Exposed for symmetry with `combined`, though no front
// end needs to read it directly today.
var combined2: int

// rtDir/haveRtDir mirror the CLI's --rtdir flag (moved from local vars to
// globals here since driveEarlySplice/driveManifestSplice both need them
// and driveCompile's own signature has no room for them): the front end
// sets haveRtDir=true and rtDir to the flag's value when given, otherwise
// leaves both at their zero value and findRtDir's own upward search fills
// rtDir in lazily, the first time a runtime module is actually needed.
var rtDir: string
var haveRtDir: bool

// haveRtbake/rtbakeBytes (runtime-ir-bake Task 4): the host CLI's
// `--rtbake FILE` opt-in, mirroring rtDir/haveRtDir's own "front end sets
// these before calling driveCompile" convention. rtbakeBytes is the raw
// '.clir' file content (main.cla reads it via file.readText, same as
// --events/--bake); haveRtbake gates the whole bake-path branch inside
// driveCompile (below) and driveManifestSplice's now-skipped call. Left
// at its zero value (false/"") by every caller that doesn't set it --
// today, that's every caller except main.cla's emit/emit68k handling.
// Deliberately NOT reset by driveReset(): like rtDir/haveRtDir, once a
// front end has resolved which bake file backs this process's compiles,
// that doesn't change compile to compile (internal/bake's DoubleCompile-
// style leak-gate variant relies on exactly this -- see its own harness).
var haveRtbake: bool
var rtbakeBytes: text

// seenPaths keys the include-once set (driver.go's `seen`), keyed by the
// path after normalizePath's lexical cleanup: "//" and "./" segments
// collapsed and "seg/.." pairs resolved, so "sub/common.cla" and
// "sub/../sub/common.cla" are the same identity. Purely lexical -- an
// absolute and a relative spelling of the same file stay distinct by design.
// The VALUE is the file's asmHeads index PLUS ONE once expand has finished
// parsing it, or 0 while its expansion is still in progress (an include
// cycle re-entering it) -- dedupHits below turns that back into an index.
var seenPaths: map of int

// asmHeads holds each expanded file's decl-list head in POST-ORDER
// (dependencies before includers), matching driver.go's files/trees order.
var asmHeads: list of int

// asmTails is parallel to asmHeads: each file's OWN last decl, recorded at
// parse time before any assembly loop links one file's tail to the next
// file's head. lastDecl() answers the same question only while a head is
// still unlinked, so any assembly that re-orders already-linked segments
// (the hoist path below) must use this instead.
var asmTails: list of int

// dedupHits records, in expansion order, the asmHeads index of every file
// whose expansion was skipped because it had already been expanded (-1 for
// a still-in-progress cycle). The runtime-module splices read the slice of
// this list their own expand() call appended: a runtime module that
// include-dedups against a file the USER already composed positionally
// needs that file's decls HOISTED out of the user chain and into the
// runtime chain, ahead of the module that needs them (extern record TYPE
// references are order-sensitive, unlike function references).
var dedupHits: list of int

// hoistedHead flags the asmHeads indices already hoisted out of the user
// chain (one entry per user-side file, filled at the end of Phase A), and
// hoistCount counts them -- a splice that hoisted nothing rebuilds the
// combined chain exactly as it always did, by appending the user chain
// wholesale.
var hoistedHead: list of bool
var hoistCount: int

// userHeadCount is asmHeads.count after Phase A -- indices below it are the
// user program's own files (hoistable), indices at or above it belong to a
// runtime module already spliced into the runtime chain (never hoisted).
var userHeadCount: int

// entryFailed records that an entry path itself could not be read -- Go's
// Expand returns this as a hard error (clarus check: stderr + exit 1). A
// front end that gets `false` back from driveCompile checks this FIRST
// (before rendering `diags`, which stays empty in this case) to reproduce
// the exact "cannot open entry file" message.
var entryFailed: bool

// dirOf returns path's directory including the trailing '/', or "" if path
// has no slash (mirrors filepath.Dir + a following Join for our purposes).
func dirOf(path: string): string {
    var i: int
    var last: int

    last = -1
    i = 0
    while i < path.length {
        if path[i] == '/' {
            last = i
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if last == -1 {
        return ""
    }
    return path[0, last + 1]
}

// baseNameNoExt returns path's final path segment with a trailing ".cla"
// stripped (if present) -- used by appinfo's filename-fallback rung.
func baseNameNoExt(path: string): string {
    var i: int
    var last: int
    var base: string

    last = -1
    i = 0
    while i < path.length {
        if path[i] == '/' {
            last = i
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    base = path[last + 1, path.length - (last + 1)]
    if base.length >= 4 and base[base.length - 4, 4] == ".cla" {
        base = base[0, base.length - 4]
    }
    return base
}

// joinPath resolves an include's path relative to dir (filepath.Join): an
// absolute name (leading '/') wins outright, otherwise it hangs off dir.
func joinPath(dir: string, name: string): string {
    if name.length > 0 and name[0] == '/' {
        return name
    }
    return dir + name
}

// normalizePath lexically cleans a path for the include-once identity
// (seenPaths) and diagnostics: collapses "//" and "./" segments and
// resolves each "seg/.." pair, preserving a leading "/" and any leading
// "../" run. Purely lexical -- no symlink, case, or cwd resolution: an
// absolute and a relative spelling of the same file stay distinct
// (documented in the reference's include section).
func normalizePath(path: string): string {
    var segs: list of string
    var work: string
    var cur: string
    var junk: string
    var out: string
    var i: int
    var abs: bool

    if not hostPaths {
        // Mac: drivePathJoin/driveKeyResolve (below) already fold every
        // "."/".." at construction time -- HFS climbs become extra leading
        // colons, key-space climbs are popped/stripped -- so a disk-form
        // or key-form identity reaching here is already lexically clean.
        // Passthrough; include dedup on Mac is exact-match on this string
        // (HFS case-insensitivity is a documented simplification, design
        // spec section 5).
        return path
    }

    abs = path.length > 0 and path[0] == '/'
    work = path + "/"
    cur = ""
    i = 0
    while i < work.length {
        if work[i] == '/' {
            if cur == ".." {
                if segs.count > 0 and segs[segs.count - 1] != ".." {
                    junk = segs.pop()
                } else if not abs {
                    segs.add("..")
                }
            } else if cur != "" and cur != "." {
                segs.add(cur)
            }
            cur = ""
        } else {
            cur = cur + work[i]
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    out = ""
    if abs {
        out = "/"
    }
    i = 0
    while i < segs.count {
        if i > 0 {
            out = out + "/"
        }
        out = out + segs[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// drivePathJoin resolves an include's relative path against dir. hostPaths:
// today's joinPath, unchanged (an absolute name wins outright, otherwise it
// hangs off dir).
//
// hostPaths=false (Mac, Task 8): rel is always '/'-separated -- design spec
// section 5: "/" is the only separator allowed in source files -- and gets
// converted to HFS ':' form at resolution time. HFS has no ".." name; its
// only "go up" notation is an empty path component (adjacent colons), so
// each LEADING ".." segment in rel becomes one extra colon appended to dir,
// and the remaining segments join with ':'. dir=="" (no directory context
// yet -- e.g. an entry file opened by a bare Standard File name) is treated
// as ":" (current-directory-relative), matching the design spec's own
// standalone conversion examples.
//
// Worked examples (task brief + design spec section 5, verbatim, plus the
// dir=="" and combined-climb cases):
//   drivePathJoin("Disk:src:", "a/b.cla")  == "Disk:src:a:b.cla"
//   drivePathJoin("", "a/b.cla")           == ":a:b.cla"
//   drivePathJoin("", "../x.cla")          == "::x.cla"
//   drivePathJoin("Disk:src:", "../x.cla") == "Disk:src::x.cla"
func drivePathJoin(dir: string, rel: string): string {
    var segs: list of string
    var cur: string
    var climbs: int
    var leadingDone: bool
    var out: string
    var base: string
    var i: int

    if hostPaths {
        return joinPath(dir, rel)
    }

    climbs = 0
    leadingDone = false
    cur = ""
    i = 0
    while i <= rel.length {
        if i == rel.length or rel[i] == '/' {
            if cur == ".." and not leadingDone {
                climbs = climbs + 1
            } else if cur != "" {
                segs.add(cur)
                leadingDone = true
            }
            cur = ""
        } else {
            cur = cur + rel[i]
        }
        i = i + 1
    }

    base = dir
    if base == "" {
        base = ":"
    }
    out = base
    i = 0
    while i < climbs {
        out = out + ":"
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < segs.count {
        if i > 0 {
            out = out + ":"
        }
        out = out + segs[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// driveMacDirOf returns diskPath's directory including the trailing ':',
// mirroring dirOf but for HFS ':'-separated disk-form paths -- used only
// when hostPaths is false, to seed drivePathJoin's `dir` argument for an
// included file's own further includes. "" if diskPath has no ':' (e.g.
// a bare entry-file name with no directory component of its own yet).
func driveMacDirOf(diskPath: string): string {
    var i: int
    var last: int

    last = -1
    i = 0
    while i < diskPath.length {
        if diskPath[i] == ':' {
            last = i
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if last == -1 {
        return ""
    }
    return diskPath[0, last + 1]
}

// driveKeyResolve computes the resource key (pure '/'-space arithmetic --
// no HFS anywhere in a key, by design: a key is a 'CLFS' resource name,
// matching the --bake key convention, never a disk path) for an include
// named rel, relative to the includer's own key baseKey. Uses dirOf
// (unconditionally '/'-based, same as normalizePath's dot-segment cleanup
// below) regardless of hostPaths -- key space is always '/' space, even
// when the driver as a whole is running in HFS disk-path mode, since a
// file's key and its disk path are independent identities threaded in
// parallel by expand(). One difference from normalizePath: a leading
// "../" run that tries to climb above the root is STRIPPED instead of
// kept -- there is no "above the root" in key space, only the flat
// baked-resource namespace a --bake invocation populated.
//
// Worked examples (task brief, verbatim, plus the resource-includer case):
//   driveKeyResolve("", "toolbox/files.cla")               == "toolbox/files.cla"
//   driveKeyResolve("", "../../toolbox/files.cla")         == "toolbox/files.cla"
//   // A file itself loaded FROM a resource carries its key as its own
//   // recorded identity (expand()'s `path`); further includes from such a
//   // file resolve against THAT key -- e.g. a baked "toolbox/files.cla"
//   // including "../osutils.cla":
//   driveKeyResolve("toolbox/files.cla", "../osutils.cla") == "osutils.cla"
//   // Both-ways case (fix round 1, review finding): a disk-based include
//   // chain and a resource-based include chain that both name the same
//   // relative target converge on the IDENTICAL key, so expand() (which
//   // now uses this key, not the disk path, as every non-entry file's
//   // identity on Mac) dedups them against each other instead of parsing
//   // the target twice -- a user file at key "src/a.cla" including
//   // "../../toolbox/files.cla", and a baked "toolbox/osutils.cla"
//   // resource including "files.cla", both land on "toolbox/files.cla":
//   driveKeyResolve("src/a.cla", "../../toolbox/files.cla") == "toolbox/files.cla"
//   driveKeyResolve("toolbox/osutils.cla", "files.cla")     == "toolbox/files.cla"
func driveKeyResolve(baseKey: string, rel: string): string {
    var segs: list of string
    var work: string
    var cur: string
    var junk: string
    var out: string
    var i: int

    work = dirOf(baseKey) + rel + "/"
    cur = ""
    i = 0
    while i < work.length {
        if work[i] == '/' {
            if cur == ".." {
                if segs.count > 0 and segs[segs.count - 1] != ".." {
                    junk = segs.pop()
                }
                // else: this ".." climbs above the root -- stripped, not
                // kept (unlike normalizePath's host arm, which preserves a
                // leading "../" run for a relative host path).
            } else if cur != "" and cur != "." {
                segs.add(cur)
            }
            cur = ""
        } else {
            cur = cur + work[i]
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    out = ""
    i = 0
    while i < segs.count {
        if i > 0 {
            out = out + "/"
        }
        out = out + segs[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// driveFreshIntList/driveFreshBoolList/driveFreshIntMap hand back a
// genuinely NEW empty container each call (same trick cg68k.cla's own
// cgFreshIntList/cgFreshBoolList use) -- required because `list of`/`map
// of` are reference types (Chapter 3: "text, list, map, and window refs
// pass by reference"), so reusing one local across several global
// assignments would alias them instead of giving each global its own
// storage.
func driveFreshIntList(): list of int {
    var l: list of int
    return l
}
func driveFreshBoolList(): list of bool {
    var l: list of bool
    return l
}
func driveFreshIntMap(): map of int {
    var m: map of int
    return m
}

// driveReset (Task 10, mac-resident-clarusc) clears every drive.cla-owned
// module global that must not leak across repeated driveCompile calls in
// the SAME process. Every prior front end (main.cla's host CLI) is
// single-shot -- one process, one driveCompile call -- so this was never
// needed before; Task 6's own report explicitly deferred it ("re-entrancy
// verification" assigned to Task 10). macgui.cla (this task) is the
// first front end that calls driveCompile more than once per process
// (once per user "Compile..." action), so it MUST call driveReset(),
// together with resetDiags() (lib.cla) and astReset() (ast.cla) -- both
// of THOSE already existed for exactly this purpose (their own doc
// comments: "call between compiling independent programs") but were only
// ever invoked once, at process start, by main.cla's startCLI -- before
// EVERY driveCompile call.
//
// memory-leak-fix Task 7: now calls libReset() FIRST, before anything
// else here -- this FLIPS the historical "lib.cla's intern pool is
// shared, unbounded-growth-but-harmless infrastructure, never reset"
// exemption this comment used to carry (see the .superpowers/sdd/
// 2026-08-12-memory-leak-fix/task-6-report.md audit). Every table that
// leaned on pool-index stability across compiles has its own reset in
// the same battery: ir.cla's irReset() (irFuncIdxByName,
// irLayoutNeededByName, irRcWalkNeededByName, ...) and check.cla's
// checkReset() already run unconditionally every driveCompile call (see
// their own doc comments), so they're covered automatically. The two
// exceptions the audit found -- rnInited (cg68k.cla) and
// shakeFuncIdxByName (shake.cla) -- did NOT have an existing reset point
// and are handled here / at shake.cla's own shakeProgram top,
// respectively: rnInit() (cg68k.cla) is guarded by a process-lifetime
// rnInited flag, so without re-arming it here, every compile after the
// first would keep using rn* globals interned against the OLD
// (pre-libReset) pool generation -- stale, silently wrong indices into
// the NEW pool. Resetting rnInited = false here forces the next
// cg68ProgramFork's rnInit() call to re-intern every rn* name fresh,
// AFTER libReset() just above, and BEFORE cg68Measure's first
// cgEmitFunc call (rnInit's own sole call site) ever needs one.
//
// Deliberately does NOT touch:
//   - ir.cla's arenas: irReset() already runs inside driveCompile itself
//     (right before lowerProgram), unconditionally, every call.
//   - check.cla/types.cla's state: checkProgram calls checkReset() at
//     its own top, every call (its own doc comment: "rebuilds the
//     universe scope... from scratch -- called by checkProgram itself,
//     callers never need to remember it").
//   - cg68k.cla's/shake.cla's own per-run lists (cgJtSlots, cgFuncLabels,
//     shakeMarks, ...): cg68k.cla's own "Module state" header documents
//     these as "rebuilt fresh at the top of every cg68Program() call"
//     (now cg68Measure, reached via driveEmit68kFork) -- reassigned, not
//     appended-to, every call. shakeFuncIdxByName is reset at the top of
//     shakeProgram itself (shake.cla, memory-leak-fix Task 7) -- same
//     reasoning, kept local to shake.cla rather than duplicated here.
//   - rtDir/haveRtDir: deliberately PRESERVED across compiles (once
//     resolved, the runtime-module directory doesn't change), same
//     reasoning findRtDir's own lazy-fill-once design already assumes.
//   - cgEventsBytes/cgBakeNames/cgBakeDatas/cgSizePartitionBytes: never
//     set by macgui.cla at all (no --events/--bake/--partition
//     equivalent for a program it compiles at runtime), so they stay at
//     their process-start zero value (empty/0) for every compile --
//     nothing to reset.
func driveReset() {
    libReset()
    rnInited = false
    seenPaths = driveFreshIntMap()
    asmHeads = driveFreshIntList()
    asmTails = driveFreshIntList()
    dedupHits = driveFreshIntList()
    hoistedHead = driveFreshBoolList()
    hoistCount = 0
    userHeadCount = 0
    entryFailed = false
    combined = -1
    combined2 = -1
    // runtime-ir-bake Task 5, fix round 3: defensive reset -- only
    // bake.cla's own bakeGenerateChain ever sets this true, and it
    // already clears it on both its own exits (success and every
    // failure return), so this is a no-op today. Added anyway so the
    // leak gate's own DoubleCompile oracle (a real cross-compile
    // correctness check, not just a style nicety) would catch a future
    // bakeGenerateChain change that left it set.
    lowSkipUiDispatchers = false
    // final-review fix wave: same rationale, same seam -- driveCompile's
    // own --rtbake branch (above in this file) already clears
    // lowResumeFromBake on both its exits, so this is a no-op today too;
    // added so the leak gate would catch a future change that left it set.
    lowResumeFromBake = false
    // fallback-trigger-narrowing Task 2: defensive reset, same rationale
    // -- driveCompile's own --rtbake branch already resets all three
    // right before Phase A runs, so this is a no-op on the ordinary
    // bake-path call sequence too; added so the leak gate would catch a
    // future change that left stale collision state across compiles.
    bkManifestDriftPath = ""
    bkManifestCollidedIdx = driveFreshIntList()
    bkManifestCollidedEarly = driveFreshBoolList()
    // object-code-linker Task 3: defensive reset, found the hard way (not
    // added preemptively) -- TestRtbakeDriftFallback crashed with "list
    // index out of range" until a reset of this kind existed. A bake
    // attempt's own per-function object staging stays populated even when
    // the SAME driveCompile call later detects manifest drift and falls
    // back to a from-source recompile (haveRtbake set false, driveReset()
    // called, THEN a recursive driveCompile() that installs no baked
    // arenas at all). Without a reset, cg68Measure's own
    // cgObjPasteEligible(i) kept reading the ABORTED bake attempt's stale
    // per-function data against the fallback compile's OWN, differently-
    // numbered irFuncs -- an out-of-bounds (or silently wrong) index.
    //
    // clir-load-perf Task 7 (design B) MOVED which global this resets:
    // it used to be `bkLdObjValid = driveFreshBoolList()`, but under
    // memoization bkLdObjValid is pending state -- the parse that filled
    // it is not re-run on compile #2, so emptying it here would silently
    // switch the whole object-code paste (stage 3.5's -41% emit68k win)
    // off from the second compile of every app session onward, with no
    // test going red (output stays byte-identical, just slower; audit
    // finding F1). bkRuntimeFuncBoundary is the LIVE per-compile half of
    // the same predicate (cgObjPasteEligible tests both), it is set by
    // bkInstallArenas on every compile that installs a baked runtime, and
    // zero here means "nothing baked is eligible" just as emphatically as
    // an empty bkLdObjValid did -- while leaving the pending parse
    // pristine. Still sufficient for the same reason as before: every
    // other bkLdObj* field is only ever read from behind a
    // cgObjPasteEligible(i) check. Safe at BOTH of this function's call
    // sites (the drift-fallback point, right before the recursive
    // driveCompile; macgui.cla's gcCompile, right before ITS OWN
    // driveCompile) -- both run strictly before any `if haveRtbake` block
    // that would legitimately re-install for the compile about to start.
    bkRuntimeFuncBoundary = 0
    // clir-load-perf Task 1: defensive reset, same rationale as the
    // resets above -- bkHeaderVerified is consume-once inside
    // bkLoadRtbake, so this is a no-op on the ordinary sequence; it
    // exists so a future aborted/reordered front-end path can never
    // leave a stale "verified" blessing for a buffer that was not.
    bkHeaderVerified = false
}

// findRtDir resolves the default --rtdir when the flag is omitted: it walks
// UP from the current working directory looking for a "runtime/clarus/"
// directory, probed by trying to read probeFile (the first needed module's
// file name) out of each candidate. Tries the cwd itself plus up to 9 parent
// levels (10 total) and returns the first candidate that has probeFile, or
// "" if none of them do -- absence here is not itself an error; the caller
// only reports one once a needed module truly can't be found.
func findRtDir(probeFile: string): string {
    var prefix: string
    var i: int
    var candidate: string
    var probe: text
    var ok: bool

    prefix = ""
    i = 0
    while i < 10 {
        candidate = prefix + "runtime/clarus/"
        probe = ""
        ok = file.readText(candidate + probeFile, probe)
        if ok {
            return candidate
        }
        prefix = prefix + "../"
        i = i + 1
    }
    return ""
}

// rtModuleKey computes the baked-resource key for a runtime module's bare
// filename (e.g. "core.cla" -> "runtime/clarus/core.cla") -- exactly the
// `--bake FILE` name scripts/build-clarusc-mac.sh gives every
// runtime/clarus/*.cla file (cliResolveBake, main.cla: "name = FILE exactly
// as passed"), so a resource-backed feReadSource (macgui.cla's) can serve a
// runtime-module read via the baked 'CLFS' resource the same way expand()
// already serves an ordinary `include`'s resource fallback (driveKeyResolve).
// On host this key is inert: main.cla's own feReadSource ignores it, always
// reading straight off disk.
func rtModuleKey(mod: string): string {
    return "runtime/clarus/" + mod
}

// lastDecl returns the last node in head's `next` chain (head must be a real
// index, not -1) -- used to splice one file's decl list onto the running
// combined list.
func lastDecl(head: int): int {
    var d: int

    d = head
    while declNext(d) != -1 {
        d = declNext(d)
    }
    return d
}

// expand loads, lexes, and parses path, recursing into its leading includes
// first (post-order decl assembly into asmHeads, pre-order parse
// diagnostics) -- a port of driver.go's inner `expand`. Returns whether path
// itself could not be read (the caller turns a missing non-entry file into a
// "cannot open included file" diagnostic at the include's position).
//
// The source read itself goes through the front end's feReadSource seam
// (Task 6) rather than a direct file.readText. `key` (a parameter as of
// Task 8, was a purely-local best-effort hint before) is the RESOLVED
// resource key for rawPath itself, computed by the CALLER before this call:
// driveCompile's entry loop passes "" (an entry file has no key -- design
// spec: the compile model is default-directory-relative, no baked
// counterpart), and this function's own include loop below passes
// driveKeyResolve(<this file's own key>, incName) for each include.
//
// hostPaths=true (host): `key` is IGNORED and recomputed exactly as before
// Task 8 -- path's tail past rtDir when path resolves inside the current
// runtime-module directory, "" otherwise -- so main.cla's front end (which
// itself ignores key entirely) sees byte-identical behavior.
//
// hostPaths=false (Mac, Task 8; identity rule fixed TWICE in review --
// round 1 closed a false-miss, round 2 closed the false-merge round 1's
// own fix introduced): rawPath is the HFS disk-form path a caller's
// drivePathJoin already built, and it's always what's threaded to
// feReadSource and to driveMacDirOf/drivePathJoin for the next level --
// design spec section 4's filesystem-first readSource() check runs
// unconditionally, regardless of how the includer itself was found.
//
// The RECORDED identity (seenPaths dedup key(s), diagnostics, pathIdx) is
// picked as follows:
//   - entry file: always its disk path. Standard File never hands back a
//     resource, so an entry is always a real disk file, and mirroring
//     host here matters -- two distinct entries must stay distinct even
//     though both are handed key="" (driveCompile: `expand(entries[i],
//     true, "")`).
//   - non-entry file, feHasKey(key) false: a plain disk file whose key
//     could NEVER be satisfied by a resource (the common case -- most
//     Mac source isn't baked). Disk-path identity, exactly like an entry.
//     Round-1's mistake was using `key` here unconditionally: two
//     UNRELATED disk files that happen to share a relative-name
//     composition (two independent entries each including a sibling
//     "helper.cla", or one entry including both "a.cla" and "../a.cla" --
//     the latter's leading ".." strips past driveKeyResolve's root, per
//     its own doc comment, landing on the same key "a.cla" as the
//     former) would silently collide and dedup away a genuinely distinct
//     file, with no diagnostic. feHasKey gates that off: neither example
//     names a baked resource, so both stay on disk-path identity and
//     never touch key-space at all -- no collision.
//   - non-entry file, feHasKey(key) true: a key a resource COULD satisfy
//     (the curated --bake namespace, e.g. "toolbox/files.cla"). Probe the
//     filesystem directly (file.readText -- a second read of a file
//     feReadSource is about to read again, same "probe before expand"
//     idiom this file already uses for runtime modules in
//     driveEarlySplice/driveManifestSplice): a disk hit registers BOTH
//     the disk path (primary/display identity -- diagnostics still show
//     the real, openable location) AND the key (a same-entry alias, so a
//     LATER include of the identical logical file that reaches it via a
//     resource-loaded includer's key-space chain still dedups against
//     it, closing round 1's original both-ways gap); a disk miss means
//     only the resource can satisfy it, so the key alone is the identity
//     (design spec's own resource-loaded-file rule, unchanged from
//     round 1).
// feHasKey (a new front-end seam function, alongside feReadSource) is
// main.cla's own "always false" (host has no baked resources); Task 10's
// macgui implements it for real (a resource-presence check, e.g.
// Get1NamedResource + immediate ReleaseResource against 'CLFS').
//
// Worked examples of what does NOT merge (round 2's fix -- both
// unreachable via feHasKey, whose real answer only Task 10 can give, so
// both stay on disk-path identity and dedup exactly like host does today):
//   Two independent entries "DirA:main.cla" and "DirB:other.cla", each
//   including sibling "helper.cla". Pre-round-2 (key-always): both compute
//   driveKeyResolve("", "helper.cla") == "helper.cla" -- same string,
//   second one silently dropped. Post-round-2: feHasKey("helper.cla") is
//   false (nobody bakes a resource literally named "helper.cla"), so both
//   take the entry/no-key branch -- path is drivePathJoin-built from each
//   include's OWN dir, "DirA:helper.cla" vs "DirB:helper.cla" -- distinct,
//   both parsed.
//   One entry "Proj:main.cla" including both "a.cla" and "../a.cla".
//   Pre-round-2: driveKeyResolve("", "a.cla") == "a.cla" and
//   driveKeyResolve("", "../a.cla") == "a.cla" too (the leading ".." has
//   nothing to pop at the root, so it's stripped per driveKeyResolve's own
//   past-root rule) -- same key, second one silently dropped, even though
//   "Proj:a.cla" and the parent directory's "a.cla" are genuinely different
//   files. Post-round-2: same feHasKey("a.cla") == false gate -- both stay
//   on disk-path identity ("Proj:a.cla" vs "Proj::a.cla", drivePathJoin's
//   HFS parent-climb form) -- distinct, both parsed.

// ---- Compile-progress instrumentation (datetime-instrumentation
// phase). Lines are emitted ONLY for want68k compiles: the C `emit`
// lane must stay byte-silent (internal/selfhost's TestErrorGoldens
// compares emit-mode CombinedOutput byte-exactly, and perfgate times
// the lane), and check-only/appinfo modes must stay silent
// (internal/reftest / internal/claruscboot assert zero output). Every
// line goes through the feProgress front-end seam -- main.cla: log()
// to stderr; macgui.cla: buffered into the Log window (painted after
// the compile until the deferred live-log phase,
// 2026-08-10-clarusc-mac-live-log-design.md).

// _TickCount (0xA975): stack-result Pascal trap -- plain `trap`, NO
// `reg` (the reg form is the documented 2026-08-03 stale-D0 bug,
// testdata/cg68k/tickprobe.cla). Host builds link rt_ext_TickCount
// (runtime/host/rt_ext_host.inc, added by this phase).
external func TickCount(): int = trap 0xA975

var drvStartTicks: int
var drvPhaseTicks: int
var drvEntryName: string

// drvSpliceActive: true while driveEarlySplice/driveManifestSplice are
// running their own expand() loop over the runtime-module list. Those
// calls pass entry=true (a missing runtime module is fatal, same as a
// missing CLI entry file -- see the fix-round-3 comments on both loops
// below), which would otherwise make expand()'s progress dispatch
// mislabel every spliced module "Compiling" instead of "Included". This
// flag lets expand() tell the two apart without touching either splice
// loop's own entry argument (entryFailed semantics are load-bearing).
var drvSpliceActive: bool

// driveProgress: timestamp-prefix msg and hand it to the front end; a
// no-op unless want68k (block comment above).
func driveProgress(msg: string) {
    var line: string

    if not want68k {
        return
    }
    line = "[" + dateTimeStr(now()) + "] "
    line = line + msg
    feProgress(line)
}

// Stage/step progress (live-log phase, 3b amendment): drive announces
// the START of each pipeline stage through feProgressStep with a
// present-tense label and a running step count -- 10 fixed stages plus,
// once cgPackProgram knows it, one per segment (drvStepsAddSegments).
// Separate from the timestamped log lines by design: log and bar are
// independent consumers of the pipeline's progress.
const drvFixedSteps: int = 10

var drvStepCur: int
var drvStepTotal: int

// driveProgressStage: announce entering the next stage. Same want68k
// gate as driveProgress -- emit/check-only/appinfo stay byte-silent.
func driveProgressStage(label: string) {
    if not want68k {
        return
    }
    drvStepCur = drvStepCur + 1
    feProgressStep(drvStepCur, drvStepTotal, label)
}

// drvStepsAddSegments: grow the step total by the just-discovered
// segment count (cg68k, after packing). The bar can jump backwards at
// this moment -- accepted by design (spec 3b).
func drvStepsAddSegments(n: int) {
    drvStepTotal = drvStepTotal + n
}

// driveProgressTick: liveness pulse from long-running inner loops
// (per-function measure/emit, per-file expansion, per-decl check).
// Callers never throttle; the macgui front end throttles by TickCount.
func driveProgressTick() {
    if not want68k {
        return
    }
    feProgressTick()
}

// driveProgressPhase: driveProgress plus a "(duration, N ticks)" suffix
// measured since the previous phase mark; advances the mark.
func driveProgressPhase(msg: string) {
    var t: int
    var d: int
    var line: string

    if not want68k {
        return
    }
    t = TickCount()
    d = t - drvPhaseTicks
    line = msg + " ("
    line = line + durationStr(d / 60)
    line = line + ", "
    line = line + numToStr(d)
    line = line + " ticks)"
    driveProgress(line)
    drvPhaseTicks = t
}

// driveProgressDone: the front ends' shared tail after a successful
// fork/image write -- the compile total plus the Finished stamp.
func driveProgressDone() {
    var t: int
    var d: int
    var line: string

    if not want68k {
        return
    }
    t = TickCount()
    d = t - drvStartTicks
    line = "Compiled " + drvEntryName
    line = line + " - "
    line = line + durationStr(d / 60)
    line = line + " ("
    line = line + numToStr(d)
    line = line + " ticks)"
    driveProgress(line)
    driveProgress("Finished")
}

func expand(rawPath: string, entry: bool, key: string): bool {
    var path: string
    var altPath: string
    var readPath: string
    var src: text
    var ok: bool
    var head: int
    var dir: string
    var d: int
    var pathIdx: int
    var incName: string
    var incResolved: string
    var incKey: string
    var missing: bool
    var tbKey: string
    var diskProbe: text
    var progressLine: string
    var key2: string
    var diskSrc: text
    var haveDiskSrc: bool
    var manifestCollision: bool
    var manifestCollisionEarly: bool
    key2 = key

    altPath = ""
    if hostPaths {
        readPath = normalizePath(rawPath)
        path = readPath
        key2 = ""
        if rtDir != "" and readPath.length > rtDir.length and readPath[0, rtDir.length] == rtDir {
            key2 = readPath[rtDir.length, readPath.length - rtDir.length]
        }
    } else if entry or not feHasKey(key2) {
        // Entry file, or a non-entry file whose key2 no resource could ever
        // satisfy: plain disk-path identity (see the function comment above
        // for why this must NOT fall through to key2-as-identity).
        readPath = rawPath
        path = readPath
    } else {
        // Non-entry, feHasKey(key2) true: could be satisfied either way.
        readPath = rawPath
        diskProbe = ""
        if file.readText(readPath, diskProbe) {
            path = readPath
            altPath = key2
        } else {
            path = key2
        }
    }

    // fallback-trigger-narrowing Task 2: a user `include` resolving to
    // one of the CURRENT bake's own manifest paths (bake.cla's own
    // bkComputeManifestPaths, computed once right after bkInstallPool,
    // before Phase A ever calls expand()) is drift-checked against the
    // baked copy's own source hash (bkManifestHashes) -- gated on
    // haveRtbake, so this whole block is a no-op (bkManifestPaths stays
    // an empty/unset map) for every from-source compile. An ALREADY-seen
    // path (seenPaths/altPath, checked here too) skips straight to the
    // ordinary dedup below instead of re-hashing/re-reading -- a repeat
    // include of the same manifest path must dedup exactly like any
    // other repeat.
    //
    // Hash EQUAL: check-only include -- let it parse into the user chain
    // normally (fall through below), so check#1 sees it and attributes
    // diagnostics to the real file, exactly like from-source's own
    // hoist-dedup does. manifestCollision/manifestCollisionEarly (read
    // below, once asmHeads.add has this file's own index) record this
    // for driveCompile's own two sever points (Task 1's decl-chain-
    // surgery mechanic, generalized to a list): the early-visible ones
    // get excised BEFORE checkPhase1 when a --testapi UI compile is
    // about to preload baked checker symbols over the SAME names (Task 1
    // Step 4's hard double-declare otherwise); ALL of them get excised
    // before lowering, since the baked IR already carries each one's
    // lowered form.
    //
    // Hash DIFFERENT (or simply absent from bkManifestHashes -- should
    // not happen for anything bkComputeManifestPaths itself found, but
    // treated the same conservative way): the existing from-source
    // fallback, now scoped to provable staleness -- bkManifestDriftPath
    // records the FIRST drifted path found, for driveCompile's own
    // post-Phase-A log line.
    diskSrc = ""
    haveDiskSrc = false
    manifestCollision = false
    manifestCollisionEarly = false
    if haveRtbake and bkManifestPaths.has(path) and not seenPaths.has(path) and not (altPath != "" and seenPaths.has(altPath)) {
        ok = feReadSource(readPath, key2, diskSrc)
        if not ok {
            if entry {
                entryFailed = true
            }
            return true
        }
        haveDiskSrc = true
        if bkManifestHashes.has(path) and bkManifestHashes[path] == bkHashText(diskSrc) {
            manifestCollision = true
            manifestCollisionEarly = bkManifestEarlyVisible.has(path) and bkManifestEarlyVisible[path]
        } else {
            if bkManifestDriftPath == "" {
                bkManifestDriftPath = path
            }
            return false
        }
    }
    if seenPaths.has(path) {
        // Record WHICH file this include deduped against (index, or -1 for
        // a file still being expanded further up the recursion), so a
        // runtime-module splice can hoist a user-composed file it needs.
        dedupHits.add(seenPaths[path] - 1)
        return false
    }
    if altPath != "" and seenPaths.has(altPath) {
        dedupHits.add(seenPaths[altPath] - 1)
        return false
    }
    seenPaths[path] = 0
    if altPath != "" {
        seenPaths[altPath] = 0
    }

    src = ""
    if haveDiskSrc {
        src = diskSrc
        ok = true
    } else {
        ok = feReadSource(readPath, key2, src)
    }
    if not ok {
        if entry {
            entryFailed = true
        }
        return true
    }

    if entry and not drvSpliceActive {
        progressLine = "Compiling " + path
    } else {
        progressLine = "Included " + path
    }
    driveProgress(progressLine)
    driveProgressTick()

    // Stamp every lex/parse diagnostic this file produces with its own path
    // (curPathIdx is read by emitDiag). Set it BEFORE lexing/parsing.
    pathIdx = intern(path)
    curPathIdx = pathIdx
    lexAll(src)
    head = parseFile()

    // Record each top-level decl's owning file so the single check pass can
    // attribute a check diagnostic to the file its decl came from (Go's
    // per-decl c.f), not the entry path.
    d = head
    while d != -1 {
        setDeclFile(d, pathIdx)
        d = declNext(d)
    }

    if hostPaths {
        dir = dirOf(readPath)
    } else {
        dir = driveMacDirOf(readPath)
    }
    d = head
    while d != -1 {
        if declKind(d) == DkInclude {
            incName = poolGet(includePath(d))
            if not hostPaths and incName.length > 0 and incName[0] == '/' {
                emitDiag(declLine(d), declCol(d), "absolute include paths are not supported on Mac")
            } else {
                incResolved = drivePathJoin(dir, incName)
                incKey = ""
                if not hostPaths {
                    incKey = driveKeyResolve(key2, incName)
                }
                missing = expand(incResolved, false, incKey)
                if missing {
                    // toolbox-include-fallback (Task 7): a dir-relative miss
                    // whose KEY (driveKeyResolve is pure '/'-space, so this
                    // check applies regardless of hostPaths) falls in the
                    // "toolbox/" key space gets one more try against the
                    // compiler's OWN toolbox/ directory, a sibling of the
                    // runtime directory rtDir sits inside
                    // (<rtdir>/../../toolbox/<rest> -- matches the relative
                    // spelling runtime/clarus/*.cla's own toolbox includes
                    // already use, e.g. uidialogs.cla's
                    // `include "../../toolbox/files.cla"` -- so a fallback
                    // hit and a runtime module's own include of the same
                    // file resolve to the identical path/key and dedup
                    // against each other via the SAME incKey passed below).
                    tbKey = driveKeyResolve(key2, incName)
                    if tbKey.length >= 8 and tbKey[0, 8] == "toolbox/" {
                        if not haveRtDir and rtDir == "" {
                            rtDir = findRtDir("core.cla")
                        }
                        if rtDir != "" {
                            missing = expand(drivePathJoin(rtDir, "../../" + incName), false, incKey)
                        }
                    }
                }
                if missing {
                    // Recursion may have moved curPathIdx into a child file;
                    // restore this file's path before stamping the diagnostic,
                    // which Go attributes to the includer.
                    curPathIdx = pathIdx
                    emitDiag(declLine(d), declCol(d), "cannot open included file \"" + incName + "\"")
                }
            }
        }
        d = declNext(d)
    }

    asmHeads.add(head)
    if head == -1 {
        asmTails.add(-1)
    } else {
        asmTails.add(lastDecl(head))
    }
    if manifestCollision {
        bkManifestCollidedIdx.add(asmHeads.count - 1)
        bkManifestCollidedEarly.add(manifestCollisionEarly)
    }
    seenPaths[path] = asmHeads.count
    if altPath != "" {
        seenPaths[altPath] = asmHeads.count
    }
    return false
}

// hoistDedups returns the asmHeads INDICES of the USER-side files that the
// dedupHits entries from index fromHit onward (i.e. the ones a single
// runtime-module expand() call just appended) resolved to, marking each
// hoisted so it is skipped when the user chain is rebuilt and never hoisted
// twice. Hits on runtime-side files are ignored: those are already in the
// runtime chain, ahead of the module that deduped against them.
func hoistDedups(fromHit: int): list of int {
    var out: list of int
    var j: int
    var hit: int

    j = fromHit
    while j < dedupHits.count {
        hit = dedupHits[j]
        if hit >= 0 and hit < userHeadCount and not hoistedHead[hit] {
            hoistedHead[hit] = true
            hoistCount = hoistCount + 1
            if asmHeads[hit] != -1 {
                out.add(hit)
            }
        }
        j = j + 1
    }
    return out
}

// driveRebuildChainSkipping (fallback-trigger-narrowing Task 2): rebuilds
// a decl chain from asmHeads[0..asmHeads.count), skipping every index
// present in skip -- the same asmHeads/asmTails relink idiom
// driveManifestSplice's own hoisted-skip loop already uses (this file,
// userChainRebuilt: walk asmHeads in order, relink kept segments via
// declListAppend, each segment's own end taken from asmTails[i] rather
// than lastDecl since a hoisted/skipped segment's own tail may already
// point past its natural end), generalized to an arbitrary caller-
// supplied skip list instead of the hoistedHead global. Every collided
// index this task tracks lives entirely within asmHeads' own range on
// the bake path (--rtbake never calls driveEarlySplice/
// driveManifestSplice, so every expand() call is user-side there), so a
// full asmHeads walk is exactly "the whole chain minus what's skipped."
// Terminates the rebuilt chain's own tail with -1, matching that idiom.
func driveRebuildChainSkipping(skip: list of int): int {
    var out: int
    var outTail: int
    var i: int
    var j: int
    var h: int
    var skipped: bool

    out = -1
    outTail = -1
    i = 0
    while i < asmHeads.count {
        skipped = false
        j = 0
        while j < skip.count {
            if skip[j] == i {
                skipped = true
            }
            j = j + 1
        }
        h = asmHeads[i]
        if h != -1 and not skipped {
            if out == -1 {
                out = h
            } else {
                outTail = declListAppend(outTail, h)
            }
            outTail = asmTails[i]
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    declListAppend(outTail, -1)
    return out
}

// driveEarlySplice implements the --testapi early splice (ui-scenario-
// retirement Task 2, ported verbatim off main.cla's old startCLI): a
// program calling any `UiTest*` name would otherwise fail the ordinary
// "clean standalone" gate just below outright, since UiTestClick and
// friends are ordinary top-level funcs declared only in uitest.cla --
// `testapi` opts a UI program (any window/menu/every decl) into splicing
// core/str/text/list/map + the ui*.cla set + uitest.cla into `combined`
// BEFORE driveCompile's own first checkProgram call, so the ONE call
// resolves every UiTest* name at once. A non-UI program, or testapi=false,
// is unaffected either way -- returns false and leaves `combined` alone.
// Returns whether it spliced (earlySpliceDone): driveManifestSplice must
// not re-add the same module set when it did.
// drvEarlyRtHeadsNoUitest/drvEarlyUitestHeads (runtime-ir-bake Task 5, fix
// round 2): driveEarlySplice's own earlyRtHeads, split into "the twelve
// non-uitest early modules" and "uitest.cla alone" (uitest.cla is always
// the LAST earlyMods entry) -- driveManifestSplice consumes both
// DIRECTLY (asmHeads-index chains, the same idiom every splice loop in
// this file already uses) to reassemble combined2 in the bake's own
// fixed order (early runtime, THEN the manifest-splice remainder, THEN
// uitest.cla, THEN user code) instead of driveEarlySplice's own
// check#1-oriented order (early runtime INCLUDING uitest.cla, then
// user). `combined` itself (check#1's own input) is UNCHANGED --
// reconstructed exactly as before, from the concatenation of both lists,
// so check#1's own visibility contract is untouched. Reset to empty at
// this function's own top (not just declared) so a stale value from a
// PRIOR compile in a long-running process (macgui.cla) never leaks into
// a compile that doesn't reach the splice below.
var drvEarlyRtHeadsNoUitest: list of int
var drvEarlyUitestHeads: list of int

// driveIsUiProgram scans declHead's own top-level decls for a window/
// menu/every declaration -- hoisted out of driveEarlySplice's own inline
// scan (runtime-ir-bake Task 5, fix round 3) so driveCompile's bake-path
// testapi branch can use the IDENTICAL check: from-source only ever
// makes testapi's early-spliced runtime visible to a UI program
// (driveEarlySplice's own original gate), but the bake path's own
// testapi install used to run unconditionally on `testapi` alone,
// diverging for a non-UI --testapi program (both in checker-symbol
// visibility -- a non-UI program could illegally resolve UiTestVerb --
// and in baked IR content, since bkInstallArenas' own testapi argument
// controlled whether uitest.cla's functions/literals stayed in the
// installed arenas at all).
func driveIsUiProgram(declHead: int): bool {
    var d: int

    d = declHead
    while d != -1 {
        if declKind(d) == DkWindow or declKind(d) == DkMenu or declKind(d) == DkEvery {
            return true
        }
        d = declNext(d)
    }
    return false
}

func driveEarlySplice(testapi: bool): bool {
    var earlyMods: list of string
    var earlyRtHeads: list of int
    var earlyTail: int
    var earlyCombined2: int
    var i: int
    var j: int
    var rtPath: string
    var beforeCount: int
    var beforeHits: int
    var hoists: list of int
    var h: int
    var freshIntList1: list of int
    var freshIntList2: list of int

    freshIntList1 = driveFreshIntList()
    freshIntList2 = driveFreshIntList()
    drvEarlyRtHeadsNoUitest = freshIntList1
    drvEarlyUitestHeads = freshIntList2

    if not testapi or combined == -1 {
        return false
    }
    if not driveIsUiProgram(combined) {
        return false
    }

    earlyMods.add("core.cla")
    earlyMods.add("str.cla")
    earlyMods.add("text.cla")
    earlyMods.add("list.cla")
    earlyMods.add("map.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uidesc.cla")
    earlyMods.add("ui.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uiwidgets.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uitext.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uitable.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uiscript.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uidialogs.cla")
    // runtime-ir-bake Task 5, fix round 2: uitest.cla STAYS in this
    // early-spliced list -- check#1 (driveCompile's own first
    // checkProgram call, over `combined`, BEFORE driveManifestSplice/
    // check#2 ever run) needs its names visible here, or a --testapi
    // program naming UiTest* fails check#1 before ever reaching
    // driveManifestSplice. driveManifestSplice now RE-POSITIONS
    // uitest.cla's own decl NODES (not a second splice -- see its own
    // doc comment) to the tail of combined2, the chain LOWERING actually
    // uses, so check#1's visibility need and lowering's own byte-layout
    // need (matching the bake's fixed order) are both satisfied from the
    // SAME one splice.
    earlyMods.add("uitest.cla")
    cpCorePorted = true
    cpStrPorted = true
    cpTextPorted = true
    cpTextArcPorted = true
    cpListPorted = true
    cpListArcPorted = true
    cpMapPorted = true
    cpMapArcPorted = true
    // map-hashtable phase Task 7: cpIntMapPorted unconditionally true here
    // too, mirroring cpMapPorted -- see driveManifestSplice's own comment.
    cpIntMapPorted = true

    if not haveRtDir {
        rtDir = findRtDir(earlyMods[0])
    }
    drvSpliceActive = true
    i = 0
    while i < earlyMods.count {
        rtPath = joinPath(rtDir, earlyMods[i])
        // Same whole-range capture as driveManifestSplice's loop below -- a
        // multi-file runtime module (one that itself `include`s another)
        // pushes more than one asmHeads entry per expand call. Files this
        // module's includes deduped against (a file the user already
        // composed positionally) push no asmHeads entry at all, so they are
        // hoisted separately, AHEAD of this module's own heads (post-order,
        // exactly where a freshly expanded include would have landed).
        beforeCount = asmHeads.count
        beforeHits = dedupHits.count
        // Fix round 3 (mac-resident-clarusc): this used to PROBE
        // feReadSource separately (into a throwaway rtProbe text, checked,
        // then discarded) before calling expand() below -- which calls
        // feReadSource AGAIN, for real, into the src this module's own
        // lex/parse actually runs on. Two consequences of that redundant
        // double read: (1) a plain inefficiency (every baked module read
        // twice, one throwaway), and (2) a real correctness gap -- a probe
        // that succeeds but whose real read then fails (rare, but a Mac
        // resource re-read is not the same operation main.cla's own
        // feReadSource -- unconditional file.readText -- guarantees
        // idempotent) previously fell straight through UNCHECKED: expand()
        // sets entryFailed and reports "missing", but nothing here ever
        // looked at either, and driveCompile's own entryFailed check runs
        // exactly once, right after Phase A's ENTRY-file loop, never again
        // after this function's own expand() calls -- so a runtime
        // module's own read failure was invisible all the way out to a
        // "successful", silently incomplete compile. Now: ONE read via
        // expand() directly, whose own return value is the single source
        // of truth for "found or not" -- removes the double-read hazard
        // entirely rather than merely detecting it after the fact.
        if expand(rtPath, true, rtModuleKey(earlyMods[i])) {
            if haveRtDir {
                abort("runtime module " + earlyMods[i] + " not found (searched " + rtDir + "); use --rtdir")
            } else {
                abort("runtime module " + earlyMods[i] + " not found (searched runtime/clarus/ from the working directory upward); use --rtdir")
            }
        }
        hoists = hoistDedups(beforeHits)
        j = 0
        while j < hoists.count {
            earlyRtHeads.add(hoists[j])
            if earlyMods[i] == "uitest.cla" {
                drvEarlyUitestHeads.add(hoists[j])
            } else {
                drvEarlyRtHeadsNoUitest.add(hoists[j])
            }
            j = j + 1
        }
        j = beforeCount
        while j < asmHeads.count {
            if asmHeads[j] != -1 {
                earlyRtHeads.add(j)
                if earlyMods[i] == "uitest.cla" {
                    drvEarlyUitestHeads.add(j)
                } else {
                    drvEarlyRtHeadsNoUitest.add(j)
                }
            }
            j = j + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    drvSpliceActive = false

    // Rebuild `combined` with the early runtime-module heads FIRST
    // (declare-before-use), followed by the original user-program chain.
    // earlyRtHeads holds asmHeads INDICES (not heads), so each segment's
    // own end comes from asmTails -- lastDecl would run off the end of a
    // HOISTED segment, whose tail Phase A already linked to the next user
    // file's head.
    earlyTail = -1
    earlyCombined2 = -1
    i = 0
    while i < earlyRtHeads.count {
        h = asmHeads[earlyRtHeads[i]]
        if earlyCombined2 == -1 {
            earlyCombined2 = h
        } else {
            earlyTail = declListAppend(earlyTail, h)
        }
        earlyTail = asmTails[earlyRtHeads[i]]
        i = i + 1
    }
    if earlyCombined2 == -1 {
        earlyCombined2 = combined
    } else if hoistCount == 0 {
        earlyTail = declListAppend(earlyTail, combined)
    } else {
        // Hoisting happened: the user chain has to be re-linked segment by
        // segment, skipping the hoisted files (now in the runtime chain
        // above) and terminating explicitly -- the last kept segment's
        // `next` still points at whatever Phase A linked after it, which
        // may be a hoisted file.
        i = 0
        while i < userHeadCount {
            h = asmHeads[i]
            if h != -1 and not hoistedHead[i] {
                earlyTail = declListAppend(earlyTail, h)
                earlyTail = asmTails[i]
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        earlyTail = declListAppend(earlyTail, -1)
    }
    combined = earlyCombined2
    return true
}

// driveManifestSplice implements the post-check runtime-module manifest
// (ported verbatim off main.cla's old startCLI, split out only for
// function-size headroom): as of runtime-ir-bake Task 2, the from-source
// path splices the full lane superset unconditionally -- core/str/text/
// list/map/sortedmap/datetime(+per-lane)/ser/ui* unless driveEarlySplice
// already spliced the early subset, plus native.cla for a 68k target
// (want68k). The old per-program usage gates (isUiProg, usesSortedMap,
// usesDateTime, usesFileSaveLoad) are retired here; tree-shaking (already
// running before codegen) drops whatever a given program doesn't reach.
// Then rebuilds `combined2` with the runtime chain first (declare-before-
// use) and re-checks it. want68k also resolves the
// app's raw name/icon path into cg68k.cla's cgAppRawName/cgAppIconPath
// globals here (Task 13, native-5e resource parity's original spot),
// since cg68BuildFork (driveEmit68kFork/driveEmit68k) needs both and
// neither reaches IR.
// `entries` is driveCompile's own entries param, threaded through only for
// the app-name fallback's baseNameNoExt(entries[0]).
//
// Sets the global combined2. Returns false on any new diagnostic (the
// front end renders `diags` itself, exactly as driveCompile's own doc
// comment promises).
// driveResolveAppName (runtime-ir-bake Task 4: extracted verbatim out of
// driveManifestSplice's own top, which used to inline this under `if
// want68k { ... }` -- pure code motion, no behavior change) resolves the
// user app's raw name/icon path into cg68k.cla's cgAppRawName/
// cgAppIconPath globals, purely from `combined` (the checked USER AST)
// and entries (driveCompile's own entries param, for the
// baseNameNoExt(entries[0]) fallback) -- never from the runtime chain.
// Needed by BOTH driveManifestSplice's own from-source want68k path
// (unchanged call below) and driveCompile's bake-path branch (which skips
// driveManifestSplice entirely -- see its own doc comment -- but still
// needs this same resolution for cg68BuildFork).
func driveResolveAppName(entries: list of string) {
    var d: int
    var appIdx: int
    var nameV: string
    var iconV: string
    var resolved: string
    var item: int
    var pname: string
    var vh: int

    d = combined
    appIdx = -1
    while d != -1 {
        if declKind(d) == DkApp { appIdx = d }
        d = declNext(d)
    }
    nameV = ""
    iconV = ""
    resolved = ""
    if appIdx != -1 {
        item = appDeclPropsHead(appIdx)
        while item != -1 {
            pname = poolGet(propertyName(item))
            vh = propertyValuesHead(item)
            if pname == "name" { nameV = poolGet(stringLitIdx(vh)) }
            if pname == "icon" { iconV = poolGet(stringLitIdx(vh)) }
            item = declNext(item)
        }
        if nameV != "" { resolved = nameV } else { resolved = poolGet(appDeclName(appIdx)) }
    }
    if resolved == "" { resolved = baseNameNoExt(entries[0]) }
    cgAppRawName = resolved
    if appIdx != -1 and iconV != "" {
        cgAppIconPath = joinPath(dirOf(poolGet(appDeclPathIdx(appIdx))), iconV)
    } else {
        cgAppIconPath = ""
    }
}

func driveManifestSplice(entries: list of string, earlySpliceDone: bool): bool {
    var neededMods: list of string
    var rtHeads: list of int
    var rtTail: int
    var rtPath: string
    var i: int
    var j: int
    var h: int
    var beforeCount: int
    var beforeHits: int
    var hoists: list of int
    var hoistBefore: int
    var earlyRuntimeChain: int
    var earlyRuntimeTail: int
    var uitestChain: int
    var uitestChainTail: int
    var userChainRebuilt: int
    var userChainRebuiltTail: int

    if want68k {
        driveResolveAppName(entries)
    }

    if not earlySpliceDone {
        cpCorePorted = true
        neededMods.add("core.cla")
        cpStrPorted = true
        neededMods.add("str.cla")
        cpTextPorted = true
        neededMods.add("text.cla")
        cpTextArcPorted = true
        cpListPorted = true
        neededMods.add("list.cla")
        cpListArcPorted = true
        cpMapPorted = true
        neededMods.add("map.cla")
        cpMapArcPorted = true
        // map-hashtable phase Task 7: cpIntMapPorted mirrors cpMapPorted
        // exactly (unconditional, no usage flag, no separate manifest
        // entry) -- intmap.cla's rtIntMap* family lives in map.cla itself,
        // which is already unconditionally spliced above.
        cpIntMapPorted = true
        // runtime-ir-bake Task 2: ui*.cla is now an unconditional manifest
        // entry (not gated on the program actually declaring a
        // window/menu/every), matching core/str/text/list/map above --
        // the from-source path splices the full lane superset so its IR
        // indices (and downstream jump-table slots/A5 offsets) match a
        // future superset bake's, and tree-shaking (already running before
        // codegen) drops whatever a given program doesn't reach, exactly
        // like it does for spliced-but-unused code today. `uitest.cla`
        // remains the one exception, staying `--testapi`-gated (it's a
        // test API surface, not runtime -- see driveEarlySplice, the only
        // place it's spliced).
        neededMods.add("uidesc.cla")
        neededMods.add("ui.cla")
        neededMods.add("uiwidgets.cla")
        neededMods.add("uitext.cla")
        neededMods.add("uitable.cla")
        neededMods.add("uiscript.cla")
        neededMods.add("uidialogs.cla")
    }
    // map-hashtable phase Task 2's cpSortedMapPorted doc comment (cprint.cla)
    // explains why sortedmap.cla used to be CONDITIONALLY spliced, mirroring
    // cpSerPorted's own old usage-gated convention; runtime-ir-bake Task 2
    // makes both unconditional (full lane superset, see the ui* comment
    // above) -- cpSortedMapPorted/cpSerPorted are set true unconditionally
    // now, same as cpIntMapPorted's own precedent (never gated at all).
    // usesSortedMap/usesFileSaveLoad/usesDateTime (check.cla) still get set
    // by the checker (no other consumer besides this now-retired splice
    // gate; harmless to leave set-but-unread) but no longer gate anything
    // here.
    cpSortedMapPorted = true
    neededMods.add("sortedmap.cla")
    // datetime (2026-08-10 spec): datetime.cla + exactly one per-lane
    // rtNow variant. No cprint ported-flag exists for this family -- the
    // builtins lower to ordinary named calls (lowCall), so the C lane
    // compiles the spliced .cla source directly.
    neededMods.add("datetime.cla")
    if want68k {
        neededMods.add("datetime_68k.cla")
    } else {
        neededMods.add("datetime_c.cla")
    }
    cpSerPorted = true
    neededMods.add("ser.cla")
    // connection (2026-08-15 serial-connection spec, Task 4/6): shared
    // conn.cla + exactly one per-lane rtConnDev* waist, same lane-split
    // shape as datetime above. Host lane STAYS usage-gated (usesConn),
    // exactly as Task 4 landed it -- nothing calls rtConnOpen/etc. from a
    // conn-less program, so gating (rather than joining the unconditional
    // full-superset splice like sortedmap/datetime/ser) keeps every
    // EXISTING (non-conn) host program's manifest, and therefore its IR
    // indices and goldens, byte-identical. The 68k lane (Task 6) DOES
    // join the unconditional full-superset splice now, matching ui*.cla's
    // own precedent: lower.cla's dispatcher synthesis (lowSynthConnDispatchers)
    // runs unconditionally under want68k too, so conn.cla's own
    // `clar_conn_fire_*` externs must always resolve on this lane,
    // dormant program or not -- conn_68k.cla is what answers conn.cla's
    // rtConnDev* calls once it's there. conn.cla itself must come after
    // core/str/text (rtPanic/rtSetLastErr, text values) and after ser.cla
    // (reuses its SerNewPtr/SerDisposePtr scratch externs) -- both
    // already spliced above unconditionally on every lane, so simply
    // appending here satisfies both regardless of which branch below is
    // taken.
    if want68k {
        neededMods.add("conn.cla")
        neededMods.add("conn_68k.cla")
    } else if usesConn {
        neededMods.add("conn.cla")
        neededMods.add("conn_c.cla")
    }
    if want68k {
        neededMods.add("native.cla")
    }

    // filehandle (2026-08-22 binary-files spec, Task 5/6): shared
    // fileh.cla + exactly one per-lane rtFhDev* waist, same lane-split
    // shape as conn.cla just above. The 68k lane (Task 6) now joins the
    // unconditional full-superset splice, matching conn.cla/conn_68k.cla's
    // own precedent: fileh.cla's own rtFh* entry points must resolve on
    // every native build once the checker's usesFileh hook can fire from
    // ANY type position (Task 5's own check.cla note, not just a global
    // var), so fileh_68k.cla must always be there to answer them, dormant
    // program or not. The host lane STAYS usesFileh-gated (Task 5's own
    // choice, unchanged) -- nothing calls rtFhOpen/etc. from a
    // filehandle-less host program, so gating keeps every EXISTING
    // (non-filehandle) host program's manifest, and therefore its IR
    // indices and goldens, byte-identical.
    if want68k {
        neededMods.add("fileh.cla")
        neededMods.add("fileh_68k.cla")
    } else if usesFileh {
        neededMods.add("fileh.cla")
        neededMods.add("fileh_c.cla")
    }

    // runtime-ir-bake Task 5, fix round 2: for testapi, combined2 gets
    // assembled from THREE separately-tracked chains -- earlyRuntimeChain
    // (the twelve non-uitest early modules, drvEarlyRtHeadsNoUitest),
    // uitestChain (uitest.cla alone, drvEarlyUitestHeads), and
    // userChainRebuilt (the ORIGINAL user files, re-walked from
    // asmHeads[0..userHeadCount) skipping anything hoisted -- by EITHER
    // driveEarlySplice's own loop or this function's own neededMods loop
    // below, hoistedHead is one shared global either way) -- instead of
    // driveEarlySplice's own check#1-oriented `combined` (early runtime
    // INCLUDING uitest.cla, THEN user). Reason: `combined`'s own fixed
    // shape (early-runtime-then-user) can't be reordered into the bake's
    // fixed order (early-runtime, then the neededMods remainder, then
    // uitest.cla, then user) by a simple append -- neededMods needs to
    // land BETWEEN the early-runtime chain and user code, not before or
    // after both, so the three pieces are tracked separately and
    // reassembled in the right order below instead. Non-testapi is
    // COMPLETELY UNCHANGED: earlySpliceDone is false, so none of this
    // runs, and combined2 is built the original way (neededMods = the
    // full 16-module list, `combined` = user code only).
    if earlySpliceDone {
        earlyRuntimeChain = -1
        earlyRuntimeTail = -1
        i = 0
        while i < drvEarlyRtHeadsNoUitest.count {
            h = asmHeads[drvEarlyRtHeadsNoUitest[i]]
            if earlyRuntimeChain == -1 {
                earlyRuntimeChain = h
            } else {
                earlyRuntimeTail = declListAppend(earlyRuntimeTail, h)
            }
            earlyRuntimeTail = asmTails[drvEarlyRtHeadsNoUitest[i]]
            i = i + 1
        }
        declListAppend(earlyRuntimeTail, -1)

        uitestChain = -1
        uitestChainTail = -1
        i = 0
        while i < drvEarlyUitestHeads.count {
            h = asmHeads[drvEarlyUitestHeads[i]]
            if uitestChain == -1 {
                uitestChain = h
            } else {
                uitestChainTail = declListAppend(uitestChainTail, h)
            }
            uitestChainTail = asmTails[drvEarlyUitestHeads[i]]
            i = i + 1
        }
        declListAppend(uitestChainTail, -1)
    }

    combined2 = combined
    if neededMods.count > 0 {
        if not haveRtDir {
            rtDir = findRtDir(neededMods[0])
        }
        hoistBefore = hoistCount
        drvSpliceActive = true
        i = 0
        while i < neededMods.count {
            rtPath = joinPath(rtDir, neededMods[i])
            beforeCount = asmHeads.count
            beforeHits = dedupHits.count
            // Fix round 3 (mac-resident-clarusc): see driveEarlySplice's
            // own doc comment (identical bug/fix, above in this file) for
            // the full reasoning -- this used to probe feReadSource
            // separately (into a throwaway rtProbe, discarded) before
            // calling expand() below (a SECOND, independent feReadSource
            // call for the same module); a probe that hit but whose real
            // read then missed silently produced a module contributing
            // zero declarations, no diagnostic, no quit. Now: ONE read via
            // expand() directly, whose own return value is the single
            // source of truth.
            if expand(rtPath, true, rtModuleKey(neededMods[i])) {
                if haveRtDir {
                    abort("runtime module " + neededMods[i] + " not found (searched " + rtDir + "); use --rtdir")
                } else {
                    abort("runtime module " + neededMods[i] + " not found (searched runtime/clarus/ from the working directory upward); use --rtdir")
                }
            }
            hoists = hoistDedups(beforeHits)
            j = 0
            while j < hoists.count {
                rtHeads.add(hoists[j])
                j = j + 1
            }
            j = beforeCount
            while j < asmHeads.count {
                if asmHeads[j] != -1 {
                    rtHeads.add(j)
                }
                j = j + 1
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        drvSpliceActive = false

        if earlySpliceDone {
            // runtime-ir-bake Task 5, fix round 2: testapi order --
            // earlyRuntimeChain, THEN rtHeads (the neededMods remainder,
            // sortedmap/datetime/ser/native), THEN uitestChain, THEN the
            // rebuilt user chain -- matching the bake's own fixed
            // internal order (bakeGenerateChain's base/uitest split,
            // bake.cla) exactly, base modules always in bakeModuleList's
            // own order followed by uitest.cla, followed by user code.
            combined2 = earlyRuntimeChain
            rtTail = -1
            if combined2 != -1 {
                rtTail = lastDecl(combined2)
            }
            i = 0
            while i < rtHeads.count {
                h = asmHeads[rtHeads[i]]
                if combined2 == -1 {
                    combined2 = h
                } else {
                    rtTail = declListAppend(rtTail, h)
                }
                rtTail = asmTails[rtHeads[i]]
                i = i + 1
            }
            if combined2 == -1 {
                combined2 = uitestChain
            } else if uitestChain != -1 {
                declListAppend(rtTail, uitestChain)
            }
            // The rebuilt user chain (asmHeads[0..userHeadCount) minus
            // whatever hoistedHead marks, from EITHER splice loop) always
            // goes last, regardless of hoisting -- hoistedHead is exactly
            // what makes this safe: a user file colliding with any
            // runtime module (early OR neededMods) is already excluded
            // here by the SAME flag that put it into the runtime chain
            // above, so it can never appear twice.
            userChainRebuilt = -1
            userChainRebuiltTail = -1
            i = 0
            while i < userHeadCount {
                h = asmHeads[i]
                if h != -1 and not hoistedHead[i] {
                    if userChainRebuilt == -1 {
                        userChainRebuilt = h
                    } else {
                        userChainRebuiltTail = declListAppend(userChainRebuiltTail, h)
                    }
                    userChainRebuiltTail = asmTails[i]
                }
                i = i + 1
            }
            declListAppend(userChainRebuiltTail, -1)
            if userChainRebuilt != -1 {
                if combined2 == -1 {
                    combined2 = userChainRebuilt
                } else {
                    declListAppend(lastDecl(combined2), userChainRebuilt)
                }
            }
        } else {
            // Non-testapi: UNCHANGED from before this task -- runtime
            // modules' heads FIRST (declare-before-use), followed by the
            // original (already fully linked) user-program chain. rtHeads
            // holds asmHeads INDICES (not heads), so each segment's own
            // end comes from asmTails -- lastDecl would run off the end
            // of a HOISTED segment, whose tail Phase A already linked to
            // the next user file's head.
            combined2 = -1
            rtTail = -1
            i = 0
            while i < rtHeads.count {
                h = asmHeads[rtHeads[i]]
                if combined2 == -1 {
                    combined2 = h
                } else {
                    rtTail = declListAppend(rtTail, h)
                }
                rtTail = asmTails[rtHeads[i]]
                i = i + 1
            }
            if combined != -1 {
                if combined2 == -1 {
                    combined2 = combined
                } else if hoistCount == hoistBefore {
                    rtTail = declListAppend(rtTail, combined)
                } else {
                    // Hoisting happened: the user chain has to be
                    // re-linked segment by segment, skipping the hoisted
                    // files (now in the runtime chain above) and
                    // terminating explicitly -- the last kept segment's
                    // `next` still points at whatever Phase A linked
                    // after it, which may be a hoisted file.
                    i = 0
                    while i < userHeadCount {
                        h = asmHeads[i]
                        if h != -1 and not hoistedHead[i] {
                            rtTail = declListAppend(rtTail, h)
                            rtTail = asmTails[i]
                        }
                        i = i + 1
                    }
                    rtTail = declListAppend(rtTail, -1)
                }
            }
        }

        // Stage 5 of drvFixedSteps. Reached on every want68k compile despite
        // living inside the neededMods.count > 0 block: the native.cla splice
        // above adds to neededMods unconditionally under want68k, so this
        // block cannot be skipped on the 68k lane -- if that splice ever
        // becomes conditional, this stage (and the step count) silently
        // vanishes with it.
        // checkProgram resets and rebuilds all check-global state itself
        // (the same reset+check sequence as the first pass). diags is
        // empty on entry (driveCompile's own first-check gate already
        // required that), so every diagnostic here is new.
        driveProgressStage("Checking Whole Program")
        checkProgram(combined2)
        if diags.count > 0 {
            return false
        }
    }
    return true
}

// driveCompile runs the whole front-end-neutral pipeline: Phase A
// include-expand/lex/parse of every entry, the --testapi early splice, the
// first (and only, for a check-only/appinfo caller) checkProgram pass,
// then -- ONLY when the front end set wantEmit -- the runtime-manifest
// splice, a second checkProgram pass, and lowerProgram.
//
// Returns false on any diagnostic (or an unreadable entry file --
// entryFailed, checked by the front end BEFORE `diags`, exactly
// reproducing the old "cannot open entry file" message's precedence).
// Diagnostics stay in the existing `diags`/`diagPaths` stores exactly as
// before this task; driveCompile itself never prints or quits -- every
// front end renders them and decides its own exit code.
func driveCompile(entries: list of string, testapi: bool): bool {
    var i: int
    var h: int
    var tail: int
    var earlySpliceDone: bool
    var wantLane: int
    var fallbackOk: bool
    var isUiProg: bool
    var earlySkip: list of int

    // findings 1.4 fix round 1: invalidate cg68k.cla's cgFindRecordByName/
    // cgSizeOf lookup caches (cgRecIdxByName, cgSizeMemo) HERE, not only on
    // cg68Measure's own 68k-lane path -- driveCompile is the one entry
    // BOTH backends share (main.cla's emitMode/appinfoMode C-print path
    // calls cgFindRecordByName straight from cprint.cla/uiblob.cla,
    // WITHOUT ever running cg68Measure/cg68ProgramFork), and it's also the
    // one macgui.cla (the Mac-resident compiler) calls repeatedly, once
    // per compiled file, in a single long-running process -- exactly the
    // multi-compile-same-process case the caches must not go stale across.
    // cgResetCodegenState's own reset of the OTHER Task 8 module globals
    // (cgMul32Used etc.) is harmless to run here too, even for a
    // check-only (wantEmit false) or C-print-only compile that never
    // touches them. The cg68Measure-side call stays (see its own doc
    // comment) -- it's still correct for the 68k lane, just redundant
    // with this one there; nothing populates either cache between this
    // call and cg68Measure's.
    cgResetCodegenState()

    // runtime-ir-bake Task 4: --rtbake path -- load+verify the baked
    // runtime image and install its intern-pool prefix BEFORE Phase A
    // (below) does any lexing/parsing of user code. This has to happen
    // before Phase A, not after check#1 like the arena install below:
    // every baked IR arena's own string-index fields (an IRFunc's name,
    // for instance) are ABSOLUTE strPool indices captured at bake time,
    // so the baked strings must occupy strPool's low indices (0..K-1)
    // before ANYTHING else calls intern() -- and Phase A's lexer does,
    // continuously, as soon as it starts. See bake.cla's own loader doc
    // comment for the two-stage install this splits into (pool now,
    // arenas + lowering counters after check#1, below) and this task's
    // report for why: check#1 for user code must run against a clean,
    // bake-uninfluenced checker (open question (b) -- the checker-symbol
    // half of the baked image is loaded but deliberately never installed
    // this task, Task 5's job).
    //
    // runtime-ir-bake Task 5: --testapi + --rtbake is now supported --
    // driveEarlySplice (which would re-expand/re-lex/re-parse ui*.cla/
    // uitest.cla as real user-chain source ON TOP of the same modules'
    // already-baked IR, a genuine double-declaration hazard) is simply
    // never called on this path any more (see the check#1 branch below);
    // --testapi instead gets the baked checker-symbol preload
    // (bkInstallCheckerSymbolsForTestapi, bake.cla).
    if haveRtbake {
        wantLane = bkLaneC
        if want68k {
            wantLane = bkLane68k
        }
        // clir-load-perf Task 7 (design B): parse the CLIR ONCE per
        // process. bkParsedValid stays true across compiles (driveReset
        // deliberately does not clear it) because the pending bkLd*/
        // bkLoaded* state is now COPIED into the live globals by every
        // bkInstall* call below, never aliased -- so a compile cannot
        // reach back and corrupt the parse it was installed from. The
        // installs themselves still run every compile, unchanged.
        if not bkParsedValid {
            if not bkLoadRtbake(rtbakeBytes, wantLane) {
                return false
            }
            bkParsedValid = true
        }
        bkInstallPool()
        bkComputeManifestPaths()
        bkManifestDriftPath = ""
        bkManifestCollidedIdx = driveFreshIntList()
        bkManifestCollidedEarly = driveFreshBoolList()
    }

    drvStartTicks = TickCount()
    drvPhaseTicks = drvStartTicks
    if entries.count > 0 {
        drvEntryName = entries[0]
    }
    drvStepCur = 0
    drvStepTotal = drvFixedSteps
    driveProgressStage("Starting Compilation")
    driveProgress("Starting")

    driveProgressStage("Parsing")
    i = 0
    while i < entries.count {
        expand(entries[i], true, "")
        i = i + 1
    }
    if entryFailed {
        return false
    }

    // fallback-trigger-narrowing Task 2: a user include collided with one
    // of the bake's own manifest paths AND its on-disk bytes no longer
    // match the baked copy's own source hash (expand()'s own haveRtbake-
    // gated check, above in this file, sets bkManifestDriftPath) -- a
    // hash-EQUAL collision no longer reaches here at all (it took the
    // check-only-include path instead, see below). Provable drift only:
    // the from-source fallback this used to trigger unconditionally on
    // ANY manifest collision now only fires when the disk copy is
    // genuinely stale, naming the drifted file so the log line is
    // actionable instead of generic.
    if haveRtbake and bkManifestDriftPath != "" {
        log("clarusc --rtbake: " + bkManifestDriftPath + " differs from the baked copy; falling back to a from-source compile")
        haveRtbake = false
        // clir-load-perf Task 7 (design B): a drifted session must
        // RE-PARSE if the bake becomes usable again -- the pending state
        // describes a runtime whose sources have since changed on disk,
        // so nothing may be installed from it again without a fresh
        // parse (and rtbakeBytes is deliberately still held: macgui only
        // frees the buffer while bkParsedValid holds, so the next
        // compile's gcResolveBakePath re-reads the resource and this
        // one's own re-parse has bytes to work from).
        bkParsedValid = false
        bkManifestDriftPath = ""
        driveReset()
        resetDiags()
        astReset()
        fallbackOk = driveCompile(entries, testapi)
        // Restored (not left false) so a LATER, unrelated compile in the
        // same long-running process (macgui.cla's Mac-resident compiler,
        // the one front end that calls driveCompile more than once) still
        // tries the bake path fresh -- this fallback is scoped to "for
        // that compile" only, per the design spec's own wording.
        haveRtbake = true
        return fallbackOk
    }
    driveProgressPhase("Parsed user program")
    // Everything expanded so far is the user program's own; everything a
    // runtime-module splice expands from here on is runtime-side.
    userHeadCount = asmHeads.count
    i = 0
    while i < userHeadCount {
        hoistedHead.add(false)
        i = i + 1
    }

    // Assemble the post-order decl lists into one chain for the single
    // checker pass.
    combined = -1
    tail = -1
    i = 0
    while i < asmHeads.count {
        h = asmHeads[i]
        if h != -1 {
            if combined == -1 {
                combined = h
            } else {
                tail = declListAppend(tail, h)
            }
            tail = lastDecl(h)
        }
        i = i + 1
    }

    // A program with ZERO top-level declarations is never what anyone meant:
    // an empty file, a file that is entirely comments, or -- the way this
    // guard was earned -- a source file the lexer silently ate whole
    // (mac-resident-clarusc Task 11: a Mac CR-line-ending .cla read by an
    // LF-only lexer, whose leading `//` comment ran to end of input). Without
    // this, such a compile SUCCEEDS: there is nothing to check, nothing to
    // splice a runtime for, shake drops everything, and the back end emits a
    // valid 12,072-byte application that does nothing -- reported as BUILT.
    // Fail loudly instead, through the ordinary diagnostic channel both front
    // ends already report. Gated on wantEmit: a comment-only snippet is a
    // legitimate CHECK-only input (docs/clarus-language-reference.md has
    // several such fences, pinned by internal/reftest) -- it is EMITTING an
    // empty application that is never intentional.
    // diags.count == 0 keeps this from piling a confusing second diagnostic
    // on top of a file that DID have declarations the parser rejected
    // (testdata/errors/varorder.cla and friends): a real parse error already
    // fails the compile with a better message.
    if wantEmit and combined == -1 and diags.count == 0 {
        emitDiag(1, 1, "no declarations found: source is empty or entirely comments")
        return false
    }

    // isUiProg (fix round 3): computed once, right after Phase A, from
    // the user's own (pre-any-splice) `combined` -- reused below both to
    // gate the bake-path testapi check-install AND, later, the
    // bkInstallArenas testapi argument, so both halves of the bake path
    // agree with from-source's own driveIsUiProgram gate (driveEarlySplice)
    // on whether this specific compile is UI-shaped.
    isUiProg = combined != -1 and driveIsUiProgram(combined)

    // Phase B: one checker pass over the whole program, collecting all
    // check-phase diagnostics (they follow every parse diagnostic).
    driveProgressStage("Checking")
    if haveRtbake and testapi and isUiProg {
        // fallback-trigger-narrowing Task 2: excise every EARLY-VISIBLE
        // check-only collision (bkManifestCollidedEarly) out of `combined`
        // BEFORE checkPhase1 runs below -- bkInstallCheckerSymbolsForTestapi
        // (right after this) preloads baked checker symbols for exactly
        // that early-visible boundary, so leaving these parsed copies in
        // `combined` would hard double-declare against the SAME names
        // (Task 1 Step 4's own probe finding). Non-early-visible
        // collisions (bkManifestCollidedEarly false) stay in `combined`
        // unchanged -- their symbols are never preloaded, so check#1 seeing
        // them is exactly case (a)'s ordinary check-only behavior.
        //
        // DEVIATION (fix round 1, task-2-report.md's own Deviations
        // section): the design brief's Interfaces line says case (b) is
        // "full dedup (no parse)". What actually happens is "dedup BEFORE
        // THE CHECKER" -- expand() (Phase A, above in this file) already
        // lexed/parsed the collided file into `combined` by the time this
        // branch runs, and only excises its decls here. A genuine no-parse
        // would have to decide case (a) vs (b) AT expand() time, but that
        // decision needs isUiProg, which is only known once Phase A (every
        // expand() call) has finished -- expand() itself cannot look
        // ahead. Behaviorally equivalent to "no parse" for every consumer
        // that matters (checker never sees these decls, lowering never
        // sees them, the parsed-then-discarded AST/pool entries are
        // exactly the check-only-include shape the leak gate already
        // proves clean) -- the only thing NOT saved is the lex/parse CPU
        // cost itself, which the corpus gate does not measure. Revisit
        // only if that cost is ever shown to matter; the fix would be
        // deferring the collision hash-check/parse decision past Phase A
        // instead of doing it inline in expand().
        earlySkip = driveFreshIntList()
        i = 0
        while i < bkManifestCollidedIdx.count {
            if bkManifestCollidedEarly[i] {
                earlySkip.add(bkManifestCollidedIdx[i])
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        if earlySkip.count > 0 {
            combined = driveRebuildChainSkipping(earlySkip)
        }
        // runtime-ir-bake Task 5, deliverable (a): the bake path's own
        // --testapi check#1 -- no driveEarlySplice (no source-level
        // re-parse of the runtime; the bake already carries its checked
        // signatures) and no combined checkProgram call. Fix round 2
        // widened the preload from a 16-name lookup to full checker-
        // symbol visibility (all thirteen early-spliced modules) via a
        // WHOLESALE funcSigs/symbols/scopes replacement
        // (bkInstallCheckerSymbolsForTestapi, bake.cla) -- which is why
        // this now runs BEFORE checkPhase1 (round 1 had it AFTER):
        // funcSigs/symbols/scopes are append-only arenas the user's own
        // checkPhase1 keeps appending to, so the wholesale replacement
        // must land first, or it would discard whatever the user's own
        // phase 1 already declared. A user name colliding with an
        // installed one is then caught by the ordinary scopeDeclare
        // call inside the USER's OWN checkPhase1 (attributed to the
        // user's file -- see bkInstallCheckerSymbolsForTestapi's own
        // doc comment for why that matches from-source's real behavior,
        // not deliverable (c)'s original assumption).
        earlySpliceDone = false
        checkReset()
        // bkInstallTypeArenaPrefix must run BEFORE checkPhase1 (user's
        // own type uses would otherwise land at the same low indices the
        // baked non-singleton types -- e.g. TyStr, `string` -- need; see
        // its own doc comment for why this is load-bearing, not just
        // tidy) and stays testapi-only: non-testapi never calls either
        // install function, so no baked type/checker-symbol index is
        // ever referenced on that path, and it must see zero runtime
        // symbols regardless (deliverable (a)'s negative test).
        bkInstallTypeArenaPrefix()
        // fallback-trigger-narrowing Task 2: same load-bearing ordering
        // requirement as bkInstallTypeArenaPrefix (must run before
        // checkPhase1) -- see bkInstallFieldInfo's own doc comment for
        // what gap this closes (a baked record type made NAMEABLE by the
        // installs around it, but not FIELD-ACCESSIBLE, without this).
        bkInstallFieldInfo()
        bkInstallCheckerSymbolsForTestapi()
        if combined != -1 {
            checkPhase1(combined)
            checkPhase2(combined)
        }
    } else {
        earlySpliceDone = driveEarlySplice(testapi)
        if combined != -1 {
            checkProgram(combined)
        }
    }
    if diags.count > 0 {
        return false
    }
    driveProgressPhase("Checked user program")

    if not wantEmit {
        return true
    }

    // runtime-ir-bake Task 4: the bake path branches here -- combined2/
    // driveManifestSplice/check#2 are all bypassed (the brief's own
    // pipeline order: load+verify stamp -> [check user, above,
    // unchanged] -> install -> lower user). irReset() first (creates the
    // singleton IRType entries at their fixed low indices, resets every
    // lazy-intern iXxxIdx guard, matches a from-source compile's own
    // reset discipline exactly), THEN bkInstallArenas() REPLACES those
    // just-reset arenas with the baked equivalents -- see bkInstallArenas'
    // own doc comment (bake.cla) for why installing the eight reverse-
    // waist dispatcher stubs verbatim would be wrong (their baked bodies
    // reflect the RUNTIME chain's own always-empty app/window/menu/every
    // state, not this program's).
    if haveRtbake {
        if want68k {
            driveResolveAppName(entries)
        }
        // Every runtime module is unconditionally in the baked chain
        // (bake.cla's bakeModuleList mirrors driveManifestSplice's own
        // now-unconditional neededMods list exactly), so these 11 C-lane
        // "ported" flags (cprint.cla) are unconditionally true here too,
        // matching what driveManifestSplice itself would set -- this
        // path never calls it, so nothing else sets them.
        cpCorePorted = true
        cpStrPorted = true
        cpTextPorted = true
        cpTextArcPorted = true
        cpListPorted = true
        cpListArcPorted = true
        cpMapPorted = true
        cpMapArcPorted = true
        cpIntMapPorted = true
        cpSortedMapPorted = true
        cpSerPorted = true
        driveProgressStage("Loading Runtime")
        driveProgress("Loading runtime")
        irReset()
        // isUiProg (fix round 3): a non-UI --testapi --rtbake compile
        // truncates the baked IR exactly like non-testapi does -- from-
        // source's own non-UI testapi build never splices uitest.cla
        // either (driveEarlySplice's own UI gate), so there is nothing
        // for a non-UI --testapi bake install to keep that a plain
        // truncated (non-testapi-shaped) install wouldn't already match.
        bkInstallArenas(testapi and isUiProg)
        // object-code-linker Task 2 used to call bkInstallObjCode() here,
        // truncating the per-function object staging to the boundary
        // bkInstallArenas just set irFuncs.count to. clir-load-perf Task
        // 7 (design B) deleted that step: the boundary is applied as a
        // bounds check by cgObjPasteEligible (cg68k.cla) instead, so the
        // parsed staging stays pristine for the next compile in this
        // session. bkInstallArenas' own bkRuntimeFuncBoundary assignment,
        // just above, is all the install this needs now.
        // fallback-trigger-narrowing Task 2: sever EVERY check-only
        // collision (bkManifestCollidedIdx, both case (a) and any case
        // (b) already excised from `combined` above) out of the chain
        // lowerProgram actually walks -- Task 1's own probe-proven drop
        // point (right here, before combined2 is ever assigned): the
        // baked IR just installed above already carries each collided
        // module's lowered form, so a user-position copy must never reach
        // lowering too, or it would double-lower the same declarations.
        // Rebuilding from asmHeads unconditionally would be a no-op-but-
        // wasteful churn on the overwhelmingly common case (no collision
        // at all), so this only replaces the plain assignment when there
        // is something to skip.
        combined2 = combined
        if bkManifestCollidedIdx.count > 0 {
            combined2 = driveRebuildChainSkipping(bkManifestCollidedIdx)
        }
        driveProgressPhase("Installed baked runtime")

        driveProgressStage("Lowering")
        lowResumeFromBake = true
        lowerProgram(combined2)
        lowResumeFromBake = false
        if diags.count > 0 {
            return false
        }
        driveProgressPhase("Lowered")
        return true
    }

    driveProgressStage("Loading Runtime")
    driveProgress("Loading runtime")
    if not driveManifestSplice(entries, earlySpliceDone) {
        return false
    }
    driveProgressPhase("Loaded runtime + checked whole program")

    driveProgressStage("Lowering")
    irReset()
    lowResumeFromBake = false
    lowerProgram(combined2)
    // native-gaps-cleanup Task 2: lowerProgram can itself emitDiag now
    // (lowCheckLiteral4CCArg/lowCheckLiteralFilter, lower.cla).
    if diags.count > 0 {
        return false
    }
    driveProgressPhase("Lowered")
    return true
}

// driveEmit68kFork (Task 9, mac-resident-clarusc) lowers the already-
// compiled program (driveCompile must have returned true first, with
// wantEmit/want68k set) all the way to a finished native 68k resource
// fork, WITHOUT the MacBinary wrap driveEmit68k below still does for the
// host `emit68k` CLI: roots the native-only entry points, synthesizes
// pascal glue for any callback, shakes, then hands off to cg68ProgramFork
// (cg68k.cla) -- the same segment-emission pipeline as driveEmit68k, just
// stopping short of the wrap+write. `nopeep` sets peep68k.cla's
// peepDisabled global, same as driveEmit68k always did; `listing` sets
// asm68k.cla's a68ListingOn global, gating listing-comment string
// construction (findings 1.8) -- the item stream is identical either way,
// only the text side table is skipped when off. On success, the
// fork bytes/app name/creator are readable via driveLastFork/
// driveLastAppName/driveLastCreator below -- a front end that writes the
// raw fork itself (`file.writeRes`, Task 10) calls this instead of
// driveEmit68k and never sees a MacBinary byte.
func driveEmit68kFork(outPath: string, listing: bool, segLimit: int, nopeep: bool): bool {
    peepDisabled = nopeep
    a68ListingOn = listing
    // native-5d Task 7: root the native-only entry points BEFORE shaking.
    cg68AddRoots()
    // Task 8 (toolbox-integration Feature C): synthesize/root one pascal
    // glue IRFunc per `callback func` an ECbAddr decay was ever built for.
    cg68SynthCbGlue()
    driveProgressStage("Shaking")
    shakeProgram()
    driveProgressPhase("Shaken")
    return cg68ProgramFork(outPath, listing, segLimit)
}

// driveEmit68k is the host `emit68k` entry point: driveEmit68kFork above,
// then the MacBinary wrap + write (cg68WriteImageWrap, cg68k.cla) --
// byte-identical to the old single-function driveEmit68k (which called
// cg68Program directly; cg68Program was itself exactly this same
// cg68ProgramFork-then-wrap sequence, now split across the two files at
// the fork seam instead of bundled in one cg68k.cla function).
func driveEmit68k(outPath: string, listing: bool, segLimit: int, nopeep: bool): bool {
    if not driveEmit68kFork(outPath, listing, segLimit, nopeep) {
        return false
    }
    return cg68WriteImageWrap(outPath)
}

// driveLastFork/driveLastAppName/driveLastCreator (Task 9, mac-resident-
// clarusc) expose driveEmit68kFork's last output to a resource-writing
// front end: the raw resource-fork image (no MacBinary header, no
// padding), the app's own declared name (appinfo naming rules -- the same
// name/appDeclName/entry-basename fallback `clarusc appinfo` uses,
// already resolved into cg68k.cla's cgAppRawName by driveManifestSplice
// above), and the four-char creator code cg68BuildFork resolved from the
// app section (declared app id, else "????"). Thin passthroughs onto
// cg68k.cla's own globals -- kept here, not there, since these are the
// documented front-end-facing contract surface (drive.cla owns the
// front-end-neutral pipeline; cg68k.cla owns codegen internals).
//
// Validity window (driveLastFork/driveLastCreator, cgLastFork/
// cgLastCreator underneath): only valid after a driveEmit68kFork call
// that returned true, and only until the NEXT driveEmit68kFork/
// driveEmit68k call. A failed driveEmit68kFork does NOT reset them --
// cgLastFork/cgLastCreator keep whatever a PRIOR successful call left
// there (cg68BuildFork, which sets both, is only ever reached after
// cg68Measure/cgPackProgram/the per-segment emission loop all already
// succeeded) -- so a caller (Task 10's front end) MUST check
// driveEmit68kFork's own return value before reading either getter, or
// risk silently reusing a stale prior build's fork.
func driveLastFork(): text {
    return cgLastFork
}
func driveLastAppName(): string {
    return cgAppRawName
}
func driveLastCreator(): string {
    return cgLastCreator
}
